But I'm definitely getting a separate battery sensor entity for every single shade with battery diagnostic "Unknown".

Would a probe-at-setup approach be acceptable here? Happy to implement if so.

Today `binary_sensor.py`'s `async_setup_entry` creates a `LutronCasetaBatterySensor` for *every* cover unconditionally. LEAP metadata can't distinguish wired from battery-powered shades (a 12V-wired Triathlon still reports `ModelNumber: "Roller Shade (battery powered)"` and a `BatteryStatus`), so filtering on metadata alone isn't viable.

**Proposal:** at setup, probe `bridge.get_battery_status(device_id)` for each cover concurrently (`asyncio.gather`, catching `BridgeResponseError`) and only create the battery sensor for devices that return a real `LevelState`. This also naturally excludes the RA3/QSX zone-keyed devices whose battery lookups 404 today (the permanent-Unknown sensors that #171829 band-aided).

Known limitation: a wired shade that *falsely reports* a battery level would still get an entity — but that's strictly no worse than today, and every 404/None case gets cleaned up. If you'd prefer a different signal (device type allowlist, config option), I'll follow your lead.

Definitely possible, but note that my wired shades *do* report a battery level of "Good", although for some reason that isn't working with this integration currently. So there might be some way to distinguish them.

That's a really useful data point — it changes the picture in two ways:

1. If wired shades genuinely report `LevelState: "Good"` when the lookup works, then battery-status probing alone can't distinguish wired from battery — the probe would only clean up the 404/None cases, not yours.
2. The reason the integration currently shows *Unknown* for your shades is probably not the shade at all: on RA3/QSX bridges, `pylutron-caseta` keys zone-devices by zone id, so `get_battery_status()` queries `/device/{zone_id}/status` — the wrong href — and 404s. That's the same underlying issue #171829 papered over. So on those bridges the probe would exclude *everything*, including real battery shades, until the library-side keying is fixed.

Which leaves the interesting question: does LEAP expose an actual power-source field anywhere in the device record? If you're up for it, could you grab the raw LEAP device JSON for one wired and one battery shade (e.g. via `pylutron-caseta`'s `get_devices()` dump, redacting serials) and paste the two records? If there's any field that differs — `PowerSource`, model suffix, capability list — that's the clean filter, and I'll happily build the fix around it. If nothing distinguishes them at the protocol level, the fallback is probably an entity-registry-friendly approach (create the sensor but mark it disabled-by-default for covers, letting users enable per device), which sidesteps guessing entirely.

Yes, this is a QSX system, so I would not be surprised if the bandaid for #171829 caused the devices to be ignored.

I don't have any battery shades, but https://github.com/home-assistant/core/issues/175900#issuecomment-4909143676 has the raw LEAP device JSON direct from the device.

